00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:19.880
Well, it depends on how you define it.

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:26.880
If you define it in the way the Agile Manifesto has defined it back then in 2001,

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:32.970
when 17 guys got together and inspired the world with their insights,

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:37.190
then I would say, yes, that kind of Agile is dead.

00:06:37.190 --> 00:06:44.130
They made a very important contribution, but in the way they have defined it, it doesn't scale.

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:48.530
And that is the big challenge because it assumes trust.

00:06:48.530 --> 00:06:53.530
It assumes respect and psychological safety.

00:06:53.530 --> 00:07:00.880
And the human mind is not wired to trust 100,000 people.

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:03.870
We cannot do that. That's just impossible.

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:08.870
We cannot feel psychologically safe in a crowd of 100,000.

00:07:08.870 --> 00:07:14.830
So Agile as defined individuals' interactions over process and tools is simply unscalable.

00:07:14.830 --> 00:07:16.830
And that was never the intention.

00:07:16.830 --> 00:07:18.830
I'm not trying to blame them for it.

00:07:18.830 --> 00:07:24.830
As they said a number of times, we were not trying to solve all the problems in the known universe.

00:07:24.830 --> 00:07:33.470
They were trying to solve problems with software projects with the specific customers, small scale, and they did that well.

00:07:33.470 --> 00:07:37.470
But now we've done that. We know how to do that.

00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:44.500
We have many organizations in the world able to release many times per day, been there, done that.

00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:48.500
Okay, we know how to do this. Well, for some, it's still a struggle, but we know how to do this.

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:53.530
The world has gone Agile. No need to talk about that kind of Agile.

00:07:53.530 --> 00:07:57.530
Now, how does it scale? Well, the Agile Manifesto has no answer to that.

00:07:57.530 --> 00:08:11.190
So from that perspective, I can agree that, yeah, Agile is dead because we need other answers, other guidance to discuss, okay, how can we work together then with 10,000 Scrum teams?

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:23.220
I mean, that somehow requires coordination. There should be some guidance on how to work with larger businesses.

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:31.530
And I sometimes see people in the Agile community say things such as don't scale or descale.

00:08:31.530 --> 00:08:37.530
And I keep saying, well, that's not an answer. There are organizations of 100,000 people.

00:08:37.530 --> 00:08:44.530
There are products and projects that require thousands of people just to be completed.

00:08:44.530 --> 00:08:50.530
You cannot say, well, let's just make that 500 Agile teams then.

00:08:50.530 --> 00:08:56.490
How do they collaborate, those 500 Agile teams? You have not answered the question.

00:08:56.490 --> 00:09:03.490
So yeah, Agile works. We've done it at small scale. Now, how does that work at scale?

00:09:03.490 --> 00:09:12.510
That's the answer to the question to answer now and that we call that business agility nowadays, not capital A Agile.

00:09:12.510 --> 00:09:18.710
And I understand why some people say Agile is dead. I would say, no, Agile is now everywhere.

00:09:18.710 --> 00:09:27.710
I sometimes compare it with cyber. When I was young, a long time ago, we talked about cyber and cyberspace and everything.

00:09:27.710 --> 00:09:32.710
And now we have cyber. It's all around us. We are now on cyber. We call it the Internet, actually.

00:09:32.710 --> 00:09:39.740
We changed the word, but it is everywhere. We're not talking about the word cyber anymore.

00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:42.740
It's a bit old fashioned to talk about that word.

00:09:42.740 --> 00:09:50.670
So I also think at some point, us old people will still be using the word Agile, but the young generations,

00:09:50.670 --> 00:09:56.670
they're not even using that word anymore because it's so obvious that you should release software multiple times per week.

00:09:56.670 --> 00:10:01.670
I mean, who comes up with the idea of doing that once per year that makes no sense whatsoever?

00:10:01.670 --> 00:10:05.240
That we have to go through that journey.

00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:15.630
And sometimes there's a critique like it's only nowadays like certificates and more the commercial thing on Agile.

00:10:15.630 --> 00:10:22.320
Maybe. What do you think about that? So is it because everyone wants to do it?

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:24.320
Maybe there should be like something like that.

00:10:24.320 --> 00:10:31.320
So it just shows that there's a need and that whenever there's a need, there will be.

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:36.540
There will be sellers of snake oil.

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:54.790
And yeah, I totally agree that the famous certification schemes where you purchase one certificate after the other, basically, by attending workshops is not the way to change organizations.

00:10:54.790 --> 00:10:58.790
I'm not saying that workshops themselves have no use.

00:10:58.790 --> 00:11:04.790
I'm organizing myself as well. I just did one here in Zurich.

00:11:04.790 --> 00:11:11.980
I didn't give anyone a certificate because I know this is useless. It makes no sense. It has no value for me.

00:11:11.980 --> 00:11:17.980
If someone wants a certificate, okay, well, whatever. I'll grab a piece of paper and I'll write something on it for them.

00:11:17.980 --> 00:11:24.070
I don't believe it's going to make any difference. I'm glad nobody asked for a certificate, by the way.

00:11:24.070 --> 00:11:33.200
I had very mature people in my workshop. So I do see the value of workshops and getting together and trying things and discussing things, of course.

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:39.200
That is interactions and of individuals and interactions over process and people. That works.

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:45.200
But the certification industry, yes, that is a bit of a pain.

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:50.200
You could say it's a parasite, perhaps, to use that kind of language.

00:11:50.200 --> 00:12:01.200
There is a lot of good stuff happening. That means there's also bad stuff trying to piggyback onto an important movement.

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:11.170
And that is what happens with such certification programs that, sadly, some people buy into, but that's just the way it is.

00:12:11.170 --> 00:12:14.890
Thank you for sharing your insights on this.

00:12:14.890 --> 00:12:21.920
What would you propose would be a better way to introduce agile business agility?

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:23.920
What do you think is the most effective way?

00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:27.670
Well, people should try things.

00:12:27.670 --> 00:12:37.670
I think they should not get a badge for sitting in a workshop for two days and writing things in a notebook

00:12:37.670 --> 00:12:43.630
that they then never open again afterwards. That has no value.

00:12:43.630 --> 00:12:50.630
What has value is if they tried something in the organization and they get a badge for that.

00:12:50.630 --> 00:12:56.260
So that is, I think, something that we need to solve somehow.

00:12:56.260 --> 00:13:03.260
I'm thinking myself about how to perhaps pursue this problem with my own work,

00:13:03.260 --> 00:13:11.260
because I don't want another certification scheme and make the same mistakes that others have made before.

00:13:11.260 --> 00:13:14.260
I'm not interested in that. I could make money with it, probably,

00:13:14.260 --> 00:13:24.410
but I would get the same amount of criticism and well-deserved if I went the same route.

00:13:24.410 --> 00:13:30.540
So I would prefer something else. If I introduce something around Unfix, for example,

00:13:30.540 --> 00:13:35.540
then it should be giving people badges for what they have done in the organization,

00:13:35.540 --> 00:13:41.540
what they have tried, what their experience is after running an experiment

00:13:41.540 --> 00:13:45.540
and what they've learned from it. That should be badge worthy.

00:13:45.540 --> 00:13:49.540
That should get you an experiment and a certificate.

00:13:49.540 --> 00:13:56.200
Yes, and as you named like the Unfix model, so maybe we can dive now inside of it.

00:13:56.200 --> 00:14:02.300
So maybe you put like the badge thing inside of it or was it just now here

00:14:02.300 --> 00:14:09.360
because you say maybe they can get a badge if they practice something good or something.

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:12.360
Is it also part of the model?

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:19.360
No, no. At this moment, there's no badges or anything yet.

00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:27.360
But I do like gamification. I do like learning from games why games are fun,

00:14:27.360 --> 00:14:33.360
while we play games and copying the useful practices from them.

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:40.360
And people like playing games and achieving levels and seeing their competence grow over time

00:14:40.360 --> 00:14:46.360
and getting them increasingly more challenging quests to solve and whatnot.

00:14:46.360 --> 00:14:52.360
You can mimic that somehow in organizational settings, I'm pretty sure.

00:14:52.360 --> 00:15:01.360
But that has been a quest for me for several years already, how to do that in an organizational setting.

00:15:01.360 --> 00:15:07.360
But I am very much a believer of gamification because games are about happiness.

00:15:07.360 --> 00:15:15.360
We literally waste our time on games. They have no benefit whatsoever other than making us happy.

00:15:15.360 --> 00:15:24.450
And that's the whole point of playing games, whether it is individual or with friends and family or whatnot.

00:15:24.450 --> 00:15:32.540
And yeah, so for me, that's inspirational. I think we should gamify learning and gamify organizational change.

00:15:32.540 --> 00:15:36.520
But I have not solved that problem yet.

00:15:36.520 --> 00:15:44.040
Okay. But I heard also in like another podcast that you compare a little bit unfixed model with Lego.

00:15:44.040 --> 00:15:52.000
So there is maybe also some parallel on gaming, maybe you can discuss or describe also for the audience.

00:15:52.000 --> 00:15:54.000
So what is maybe the unfixed model?

00:15:54.000 --> 00:16:04.220
Right. So I started with the observation that pictures are popular, apparently in our community,

00:16:04.220 --> 00:16:12.220
whether it is a picture of the scaled agile framework or a large scale scrum or whatever, pictures draw attention.

00:16:12.220 --> 00:16:20.220
That was one observation. Another one is that in terms of organization design, we do not have a lot to choose from.

00:16:20.220 --> 00:16:27.280
SAFE has some suggestions on how to structure yourself, but it is not complete.

00:16:27.280 --> 00:16:35.240
It does not address HR or marketing or finance. So it focuses on product development.

00:16:35.240 --> 00:16:43.390
That is basically the idea. And then there are others that have a similar approach,

00:16:43.390 --> 00:16:47.390
whether it is LESS or Nexus or Scrum at scale. It is all about product development.

00:16:47.390 --> 00:16:53.390
They ignore the rest of the company and are, in my opinion, very weak in terms of organization design.

00:16:53.390 --> 00:17:01.390
And then we have others such as Holacracy that discuss organization design from a more holistic perspective,

00:17:01.390 --> 00:17:07.390
but they are so wacky and so extreme that no sensible manager would ever implement something like that.

00:17:07.390 --> 00:17:16.610
They are too off the rails as far as I'm concerned. So, and that's basically, those are the options.

00:17:16.610 --> 00:17:24.610
Oh, and then we have the Spotify model, which was actually a case study that people now use as if it was a framework.

00:17:24.610 --> 00:17:33.610
Well, it's interesting to be inspired by, but it was 10 years ago and the company has grown 10 times larger since then.

00:17:33.610 --> 00:17:41.640
And they're not doing what that was described 10 years ago anymore. So those are the best options we have.

00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:50.890
So I thought we need something else. That was my observation. The market apparently wants to be inspired by organizational models.

00:17:50.890 --> 00:18:02.300
How to structure ourselves to answer that question of how do we get those 500 scrum teams to collaborate with each other?

00:18:02.300 --> 00:18:07.300
Because we are a large organization of 20,000 people or something like that.

00:18:07.300 --> 00:18:14.260
We need answers to that in a way that also addresses marketing and sales and HR and you name it.

00:18:14.260 --> 00:18:20.260
So I decided, okay, let's go after that. Let's make organization design my thing.

00:18:20.260 --> 00:18:28.260
Let's create pictures because people like pictures that draw attention and it helps in terms of visualization.

00:18:28.260 --> 00:18:38.430
People need that. And what I want to be different from frameworks is the word framework suggests that there is an essential structure.

00:18:38.430 --> 00:18:47.490
That is the definition of the word framework, that you have something static that you cannot change and that supports everything else.

00:18:47.490 --> 00:18:52.550
If you remove the framework, then you have no framework anymore if everything is optional.

00:18:52.550 --> 00:19:00.550
And I do not like that approach. I do not like the idea that you have a minimal set of practices.

00:19:00.550 --> 00:19:09.670
For me, everything is optional. And that's why I use the metaphor of Lego, because with a Lego box, everything is optional.

00:19:09.670 --> 00:19:16.670
You begin with a box full of blocks and an empty desk. That's your starting point.

00:19:16.670 --> 00:19:19.670
And then you grab the blocks that you want and you start building.

00:19:19.670 --> 00:19:26.950
And the better description then would actually be a pattern library of what Unfix is.

00:19:26.950 --> 00:19:34.000
It is a collection of good patterns that I found in several sources and I credit them all.

00:19:34.000 --> 00:19:39.990
And I leave it to people to start building with the blocks.

00:19:39.990 --> 00:19:46.990
So Lego is for me a metaphor that helps people understand how to use this model.

00:19:46.990 --> 00:19:49.020
It is not a framework.

00:19:49.020 --> 00:19:52.020
People easily, particularly in the Agile community,

00:19:52.020 --> 00:19:55.020
they call just about everything a framework nowadays,

00:19:55.020 --> 00:19:59.020
which just means that they don't even understand what the word means.

00:19:59.020 --> 00:20:03.040
Because many things that they call a framework are not a framework.

00:20:03.040 --> 00:20:06.480
Some people say the same thing about management 3.0.

00:20:06.480 --> 00:20:08.480
Well, that's also not a framework.

00:20:08.480 --> 00:20:11.480
Nothing is mandatory with management 3.0.

00:20:11.480 --> 00:20:16.480
They're just useful practices for managers and leaders.

00:20:16.480 --> 00:20:21.670
So a pattern library is the better descriptor in my opinion.

00:20:21.670 --> 00:20:24.950
Yes. And I guess in organization,

00:20:24.950 --> 00:20:30.140
there's not like a blueprint you can put on every organization.

00:20:30.140 --> 00:20:32.140
It's more like you pick whatever you need,

00:20:32.140 --> 00:20:34.140
maybe your employees need.

00:20:34.140 --> 00:20:36.330
And there's also like an unfix.

00:20:36.330 --> 00:20:44.830
So you collect maybe the Lego bricks like you need for your organization

00:20:44.830 --> 00:20:48.830
and maybe some you don't need and you just pick them.

00:20:48.830 --> 00:20:50.730
Yeah, exactly.

00:20:50.730 --> 00:20:57.720
And well, one well-known pattern is of course the value stream team,

00:20:57.720 --> 00:21:02.720
whether it is a Scrum team or Kandam team or feature team or Agile team.

00:21:02.720 --> 00:21:03.720
It doesn't matter what you call them.

00:21:03.720 --> 00:21:06.720
It is the same pattern as far as I'm concerned.

00:21:06.720 --> 00:21:10.690
And I call that the value stream crew actually.

00:21:10.690 --> 00:21:15.690
I like the word crew because it signals that this is a team with a mission

00:21:15.690 --> 00:21:19.720
that they're going from one place to the other to get something done.

00:21:19.720 --> 00:21:24.680
But team is also a fine word if that's what you prefer.

00:21:24.680 --> 00:21:28.680
And that's just one very common pattern, at least in the Agile community.

00:21:28.680 --> 00:21:30.680
We know this pattern.

00:21:30.680 --> 00:21:32.680
There are other patterns as well.

00:21:32.680 --> 00:21:35.680
Facilitation crew and platform crew and governance crew and others.

00:21:35.680 --> 00:21:41.640
And it's up to you to combine them in such a way that they make sense for you.

00:21:41.640 --> 00:21:44.640
But the Lego blocks come with constraints.

00:21:44.640 --> 00:21:49.640
Like you know from Lego, you cannot put them together in any way, random way.

00:21:49.640 --> 00:21:54.780
They only fit in a certain way, fit together in a certain manner.

00:21:54.780 --> 00:21:59.900
That is by design. That is why it is also fun to play with it.

00:21:59.900 --> 00:22:03.020
And the same applies to Unfix.

00:22:03.020 --> 00:22:08.990
It is by design that you cannot just make everything.

00:22:08.990 --> 00:22:13.990
I don't want people to create traditional companies with it.

00:22:13.990 --> 00:22:16.990
That is not allowed. That is not the idea.

00:22:16.990 --> 00:22:23.080
You should make modern businesses with the practices, the patterns of the future and not of the past.

00:22:23.080 --> 00:22:35.130
And as you mentioned up front, the challenge of scaling is one which is probably still existing.

00:22:35.130 --> 00:22:42.240
If you look at the unfixed model, how does this actually fit in, the question of scaling up?

00:22:42.240 --> 00:22:48.240
So, yeah, good question. I actually sort of divided the problem in two.

00:22:48.240 --> 00:22:52.240
There's one very important pattern that I call the base.

00:22:52.240 --> 00:22:56.240
You could call it the tribe or the business unit.

00:22:56.240 --> 00:23:01.240
I actually borrowed that from the Spotify model where they also have a tribe concept.

00:23:01.240 --> 00:23:05.240
It does not really exist in other models.

00:23:05.240 --> 00:23:10.240
It is not exactly the same as an art in the Scaled Agile Framework, for example.

00:23:10.240 --> 00:23:17.360
Because in SAFE they assume that an art creates one thing together with multiple teams.

00:23:17.360 --> 00:23:22.510
But if you have tiny products, you don't need SAFE.

00:23:22.510 --> 00:23:26.510
You just have four individual teams doing four individual Scrum projects.

00:23:26.510 --> 00:23:32.610
But you would still benefit from having a base, as I call it, a tribe.

00:23:32.610 --> 00:23:35.610
Something that people feel they belong to.

00:23:35.610 --> 00:23:47.210
So those four individual Scrum teams, they would benefit from feeling part of a larger tribe, a base, as they also said in the Spotify model.

00:23:47.210 --> 00:23:50.210
So I agree with that because we need a sense of belonging.

00:23:50.210 --> 00:23:55.210
We need that sense of psychological safety and recognition, and it doesn't scale.

00:23:55.210 --> 00:24:04.330
So I say anything in the base is the stuff that we do traditionally with Agile as defined in the Agile Manifesto.

00:24:04.330 --> 00:24:12.290
It doesn't scale. It scales up to the boundaries of the base of that tribe. We know how to do that.

00:24:12.290 --> 00:24:19.290
But if your company exists of 10,000 people, you're going to need multiple bases.

00:24:19.290 --> 00:24:28.950
And each base could be organized on the inside in a very different way with different combinations of Scrum teams or Kanban teams or whatever you prefer.

00:24:28.950 --> 00:24:39.000
It depends on their objectives, on their jobs. And then the question is, how do those bases work together?

00:24:39.000 --> 00:24:44.000
And that is interesting because then you have similar patterns.

00:24:44.000 --> 00:24:55.060
You still have value streams of multiple bases, for example. You can have a facilitation cluster of multiple bases, etc.

00:24:55.060 --> 00:25:04.120
But probably you're going to need some rules and tools and procedures for having them work together because that's a lot of people.

00:25:04.120 --> 00:25:11.110
They will have to rely more on defined procedures and processes for how to work together.

00:25:11.110 --> 00:25:19.110
I know one company that I often mention, which is Hire, a company in China that I visited a good number of years ago.

00:25:19.110 --> 00:25:29.110
They have 4,000 micro-enterprises. Imagine that, 4,000 of these bases. And each one is about 15 people on average.

00:25:29.110 --> 00:25:36.040
So you can imagine within such a micro-enterprise, within those 15 people, they can be agile as in the Agile Manifesto.

00:25:36.040 --> 00:25:45.350
But among the 4,000 micro-enterprises, they do contracting, I assure you. They sign contracts with each other. They have to.

00:25:45.350 --> 00:25:53.570
Because you cannot just do everything in a self-organized, anarchistic way across 80,000 people. That doesn't work.

00:25:53.570 --> 00:26:02.590
So that's how you split the two, right? Within the base or the micro-enterprise, you have Agile, the traditional sense.

00:26:02.590 --> 00:26:09.620
That's where the Agile Manifesto applies, individual interactions over process tools, et cetera, et cetera.

00:26:09.620 --> 00:26:20.620
Beyond the base level, you're going to deal with people you never knew before, that you don't necessarily trust immediately.

00:26:20.620 --> 00:26:25.620
Because they're from a completely different part of the company, maybe even another country or whatever.

00:26:25.620 --> 00:26:36.800
So you need a more formal approach. But interestingly enough, the patterns are very similar across that boundary.

00:26:36.800 --> 00:26:44.070
And that's why I call it a fractal model, because the same patterns repeat themselves at the next level up.

00:26:44.070 --> 00:26:52.790
Which I think is also typical systemic concept, right, that the patterns definitely reproduce themselves on some different levels.

00:26:52.790 --> 00:27:00.010
I like this very much, especially because I know that one of our businesses segments overall in our company,

00:27:00.010 --> 00:27:07.000
we have over 100,000 employees, one of our segments, I think around 20,000 people try to, or are introducing the,

00:27:07.000 --> 00:27:15.180
I'll call it the Spotify model, more or less, with maybe slight adjustments and exactly are struggling,

00:27:15.180 --> 00:27:20.180
or were struggling quite a bit with the topic of scaling and, you know, have exactly this thought.

00:27:20.180 --> 00:27:25.180
And I liked the way they introduced it with the top, with the boards being really involved, really,

00:27:25.180 --> 00:27:31.220
you know, going on the journeys change, starting with me, basically this idea in the head of the board

00:27:31.220 --> 00:27:37.390
and transferring it to the corporate part of the segment, but really going into the depth and really

00:27:37.390 --> 00:27:47.350
to let me see the blue color. This is something which I think at least from my observations did not work very well.

00:27:47.350 --> 00:27:52.350
Do you have examples, maybe, or recommendations, what could help? And does it even make sense

00:27:52.350 --> 00:27:57.700
to maybe even go on a blue color level in production or similar settings?

00:27:57.700 --> 00:28:07.880
Well, to be honest, my frame of reference is companies such as Hire and PipeDrive

00:28:07.880 --> 00:28:15.880
and others that... I have a case study written where they have, again, tribes, as I suggest,

00:28:15.880 --> 00:28:21.840
and they call them tribes, I call them bases, but it's the same thing.

00:28:21.840 --> 00:28:26.900
And I am pretty much convinced that the same principles apply everywhere.

00:28:26.900 --> 00:28:30.900
I just do not have experience with what, as you said, blue collar work.

00:28:30.900 --> 00:28:35.900
I mean, I'm a tech person. That's where I am from. I'm a software engineer.

00:28:35.900 --> 00:28:40.900
I work with Agile coaches and consultants who are my clients and they work

00:28:40.900 --> 00:28:46.090
with software engineering projects and products.

00:28:46.090 --> 00:28:50.120
So that is the context that I operate in.

00:28:50.120 --> 00:28:54.120
But that being said, I do believe that it applies elsewhere

00:28:54.120 --> 00:29:00.120
because nothing in the patterns suggests software per se.

00:29:00.120 --> 00:29:05.270
I do believe, however, that the combination of the building blocks

00:29:05.270 --> 00:29:08.270
will be very different in different contexts.

00:29:08.270 --> 00:29:15.270
So, for example, I use as an analogy sometimes

00:29:15.270 --> 00:29:19.270
the airline industry or hospitals or fire departments

00:29:19.270 --> 00:29:25.270
to show that reteaming is a normal thing in other industries.

00:29:25.270 --> 00:29:30.360
And it's something that we are in the agile community still a bit scared of.

00:29:30.360 --> 00:29:34.360
Traditionally, we have always said don't change your teams

00:29:34.360 --> 00:29:36.360
because your velocity will drop.

00:29:36.360 --> 00:29:38.360
It will hurt.

00:29:38.360 --> 00:29:41.360
It will hurt your throughput and everything.

00:29:41.360 --> 00:29:45.360
And I said, well, in other industries they do this all the time, reteaming,

00:29:45.360 --> 00:29:47.360
and they figured out how to do it.

00:29:47.360 --> 00:29:52.520
So, instead of saying don't do it, maybe we should just learn how to do it

00:29:52.520 --> 00:29:58.540
so we can enjoy the benefits of reteaming because there are quite some benefits.

00:29:58.540 --> 00:30:01.540
And there are companies who have done this now.

00:30:01.540 --> 00:30:03.540
They do this at Redgate Software.

00:30:03.540 --> 00:30:06.540
They do this at Pipedrive.

00:30:06.540 --> 00:30:09.540
There's even an Agile Framework, the FAST Framework,

00:30:09.540 --> 00:30:12.570
where they use an open space kind of principle

00:30:12.570 --> 00:30:15.720
to let people flock towards the sprint goals.

00:30:15.720 --> 00:30:17.720
And every sprint the teams are different.

00:30:17.720 --> 00:30:18.720
They make it work.

00:30:18.720 --> 00:30:23.720
So, but it took us 20 years to figure out that reteaming

00:30:23.720 --> 00:30:28.840
can actually be made to work in the Agile community as well.

00:30:28.840 --> 00:30:32.970
And that's something that already exists in other industries.

00:30:32.970 --> 00:30:36.970
So that's, as an example, I'm just showing you this,

00:30:36.970 --> 00:30:41.970
that the patterns translates across industry boundaries, I'm pretty sure.

00:30:41.970 --> 00:30:45.970
It's just the combination of the building blocks will be different.

00:30:45.970 --> 00:30:48.970
Like you can use the same building blocks to build spaceships

00:30:48.970 --> 00:30:50.970
and to build castles.

00:30:50.970 --> 00:30:53.070
Those are the same blocks.

00:30:53.070 --> 00:30:56.130
The picture is completely different.

00:30:56.130 --> 00:31:00.130
So that depends on the context, what you want to make out of it.

00:31:00.130 --> 00:31:04.130
It depends on the function, but the patterns, the building blocks are the same.

00:31:04.130 --> 00:31:06.630
I like that idea very, very much.

00:31:06.630 --> 00:31:11.630
And a question which comes to mind is, especially based on your experience

00:31:11.630 --> 00:31:16.630
going through over the whole world to implement this idea of these thoughts

00:31:16.630 --> 00:31:22.040
and these approaches, what kind of influence from cultures do you actually see?

00:31:22.040 --> 00:31:24.040
Are they very big?

00:31:24.040 --> 00:31:28.040
Is there maybe some cultures which are maybe more,

00:31:28.040 --> 00:31:31.040
which maybe work on this kind of approach easier

00:31:31.040 --> 00:31:33.040
or have been more challenges or difficulties?

00:31:33.040 --> 00:31:36.040
What are your experience in this regard?

00:31:36.040 --> 00:31:43.830
Well, I always say that geographical culture or regional culture

00:31:43.830 --> 00:31:46.830
is just one dimension of culture.

00:31:46.830 --> 00:31:48.830
We also have professional culture.

00:31:48.830 --> 00:31:51.830
We have industry culture.

00:31:51.830 --> 00:31:55.790
It makes quite a difference whether you work in the banking industry

00:31:55.790 --> 00:31:59.790
or whether you work in the media industry.

00:31:59.790 --> 00:32:01.790
Those are completely different worlds.

00:32:01.790 --> 00:32:06.790
And I think a bank in Germany versus a bank in Brazil

00:32:06.790 --> 00:32:10.790
are much more similar to each other than a bank in Germany

00:32:10.790 --> 00:32:14.790
versus a media company in Germany, right?

00:32:14.790 --> 00:32:17.790
Because that's the industry dimension of culture,

00:32:17.790 --> 00:32:21.790
which has much more an impact on organizational change

00:32:21.790 --> 00:32:23.790
and people's behaviors.

00:32:23.790 --> 00:32:26.790
So let's not forget about that.

00:32:26.790 --> 00:32:30.790
But of course, there is a geographical dimension for sure.

00:32:30.790 --> 00:32:33.790
And there's a ton of research into that.

00:32:33.790 --> 00:32:37.790
We know about power distance being lower

00:32:37.790 --> 00:32:42.790
in the Northwestern European countries compared to East and South.

00:32:42.790 --> 00:32:44.790
Things like that.

00:32:44.790 --> 00:32:50.790
We know that the social dimension is bigger in the Far East of the world

00:32:50.790 --> 00:32:55.790
compared to the individualism in Europe and the US.

00:32:55.790 --> 00:32:57.790
These are known things.

00:32:57.790 --> 00:33:04.040
Yes, they have an impact on how organizations change.

00:33:04.040 --> 00:33:08.040
But the details I'm not familiar with.

00:33:08.040 --> 00:33:10.040
I'm not a coach or consultant.

00:33:10.040 --> 00:33:13.040
I don't go into organizations to help them change.

00:33:13.040 --> 00:33:14.040
I leave that to others.

00:33:14.040 --> 00:33:18.040
My clients actually are coaches and consultants usually.

00:33:18.040 --> 00:33:24.070
And I am the one who flies at the 30,000 feet view as I often say.

00:33:24.070 --> 00:33:29.190
I see the more abstract patterns that sort of apply everywhere.

00:33:29.190 --> 00:33:32.190
Because ultimately we're all human beings.

00:33:32.190 --> 00:33:39.150
Ultimately we're emotional, very irrational animals.

00:33:39.150 --> 00:33:44.150
And the differences are in the details as far as I'm concerned.

00:33:44.150 --> 00:33:48.150
From that high level that I am looking at things.

00:33:48.150 --> 00:34:00.050
And yes, if there is like maybe from the audience someone who wants to put this unfixed model in their organization.

00:34:00.050 --> 00:34:06.740
Is there, how could they start something maybe just contact you or I don't know.

00:34:06.740 --> 00:34:11.740
Yeah, well, there is a website unfix.work where you find everything.

00:34:11.740 --> 00:34:20.740
There's a community that we have a mirror board with the elements, the patterns that you can use.

00:34:20.740 --> 00:34:26.740
You start drawing things as a PowerPoint template and individual images, et cetera, et cetera.

00:34:26.740 --> 00:34:30.740
So you can do drawing. And that's what I suggest as a starting point.

00:34:30.740 --> 00:34:34.870
Just start drawing things. You don't have to change anything.

00:34:34.870 --> 00:34:40.050
Just make pictures of how the future could be.

00:34:40.050 --> 00:34:46.050
And that is very similar to what they call scenario planning in business strategy.

00:34:46.050 --> 00:34:51.050
You envision multiple futures and say, well, we could go in this direction.

00:34:51.050 --> 00:34:53.050
It could go in that direction.

00:34:53.050 --> 00:35:00.180
Maybe have three, four, five potential pictures of how the organization could work.

00:35:00.180 --> 00:35:06.180
Build with the unfixed building blocks, the patterns.

00:35:06.180 --> 00:35:09.180
And that's your starting point to have that conversation.

00:35:09.180 --> 00:35:12.180
What are the things that will have to change?

00:35:12.180 --> 00:35:17.180
And then you will probably see that some changes will be easier than others.

00:35:17.180 --> 00:35:28.170
It is pretty easy to start a forum or a guild compared to moving managers out of teams, for example,

00:35:28.170 --> 00:35:35.130
which requires HR to be involved and requires people giving up territories and stuff.

00:35:35.130 --> 00:35:38.130
So it's probably not the best starting point.

00:35:38.130 --> 00:35:45.190
I advise against having a manager on a scrum team, for example.

00:35:45.190 --> 00:35:48.220
The whole Agile community seems to be against that concept.

00:35:48.220 --> 00:35:55.220
And I agree with that because you don't have a territory and managers have territories and they don't easily like to give them up.

00:35:55.220 --> 00:36:01.220
So it makes it hard to change things when you have territories that people try to protect.

00:36:01.220 --> 00:36:07.440
So but if you have that, then, yeah, you'll have to do something about it and that might be hard.

00:36:07.440 --> 00:36:11.410
So start with another pattern that is that is easier.

00:36:11.410 --> 00:36:14.410
And you mentioned the Spotify model earlier.

00:36:14.410 --> 00:36:25.780
There are similarities, of course, but one big difference is is the matrix structure, which is embedded in the Spotify model.

00:36:25.780 --> 00:36:37.060
It has these the what is the chapters with the chapter leads who are by default the managers of all the engineers in their chapter.

00:36:37.060 --> 00:36:46.220
And at the same time, those those engineers also discuss products with with product owners.

00:36:46.220 --> 00:36:52.180
And then you have sort of two people to talk to throughout the week or the month.

00:36:52.180 --> 00:36:57.180
And that that is that is a matrix organization that can lead to problems.

00:36:57.180 --> 00:37:02.180
Organization design literature is full of warnings against matrix structures.

00:37:02.180 --> 00:37:06.180
They lead to big fat middle management layers.

00:37:06.180 --> 00:37:18.180
They lead to more escalations bubbling up the hierarchy because the managers, the line managers and the product managers cannot agree on the solution of someone's availability.

00:37:18.180 --> 00:37:23.210
And then things bubble up. It's a pain at some point.

00:37:23.210 --> 00:37:28.210
Not everyone struggles with it. I have seen some companies that are doing well with a matrix.

00:37:28.210 --> 00:37:31.210
And then in that case, I would say, well, don't change the working program.

00:37:31.210 --> 00:37:34.530
If it works for you, fine.

00:37:34.530 --> 00:37:43.530
But I foresee that this could become an issue when you get larger, because when the matrix gets larger horizontally and vertically,

00:37:43.530 --> 00:37:50.530
if you double the number of people, you quadruple the number of connections between them in the matrix.

00:37:50.530 --> 00:37:55.620
And that is an issue in matrix structure.

00:37:55.620 --> 00:38:02.580
So that is difficult to solve, actually. That is painful if you want to change that.

00:38:02.580 --> 00:38:05.530
That is probably not a starting point.

00:38:05.530 --> 00:38:12.210
Probably you want to start somewhere else, changing, introducing the ideas.

00:38:12.210 --> 00:38:19.190
And is it, as you said, like maybe it's more easy to start with a guild or something.

00:38:19.190 --> 00:38:27.190
So would you also say like if we organization want to change something and maybe until the unfixed model,

00:38:27.190 --> 00:38:32.160
to start small or a little bit? I don't know.

00:38:32.160 --> 00:38:36.250
I always suggest that that's from systems theory, complexity, science.

00:38:36.250 --> 00:38:41.250
You cannot make a complex system, a large complex system from scratch.

00:38:41.250 --> 00:38:48.240
You have to start all over and begin with a small system that works and then steadily grow it over time.

00:38:48.240 --> 00:38:52.240
It's the same with organization design changes.

00:38:52.240 --> 00:38:57.240
I just discussed it earlier today on another webinar about change management in general.

00:38:57.240 --> 00:39:04.240
You'll need to start with a low hanging fruit, small changes, then gradually build up over time.

00:39:04.240 --> 00:39:09.240
And then, yes, a guild is a relatively painless thing to begin with.

00:39:09.240 --> 00:39:15.240
I mean, just get people together across different teams and have them have a conversation over a coffee,

00:39:15.240 --> 00:39:21.240
preferably outside of the office in another environment so that it's easier to share opinions

00:39:21.240 --> 00:39:31.620
and thoughts and perspectives. And an unfixed guild or forum would be a great beginning, I suppose,

00:39:31.620 --> 00:39:34.620
where people discuss what could the organization look like?

00:39:34.620 --> 00:39:41.590
What would be necessary to turn this into a base instead of a matrix?

00:39:41.590 --> 00:39:45.590
What different small steps would that require?

00:39:45.590 --> 00:39:50.940
And, yeah, that would be definitely my suggestion.

00:39:50.940 --> 00:40:01.600
And maybe you also then catch the employees because I see it and maybe I'm wrong, but I always see like in change projects,

00:40:01.600 --> 00:40:07.880
they change a lot as a structure, whatever, but they have like a big strategy.

00:40:07.880 --> 00:40:16.970
And in the end, they see like, okay, but we didn't put the execution thing or the employees inside of it.

00:40:16.970 --> 00:40:18.970
So they don't take them with us.

00:40:18.970 --> 00:40:28.620
Yeah, well, I think organization restructuring also benefits from experiments.

00:40:28.620 --> 00:40:40.160
And as far as I understand, even that big Chinese company, Hire, that is famous for firing thousands of managers, middle managers,

00:40:40.160 --> 00:40:48.130
and basically requiring them to become a manager of a micro enterprise or find a job somewhere else.

00:40:48.130 --> 00:40:53.130
That's what they did like 10 plus years ago. They began with experiments.

00:40:53.130 --> 00:40:58.130
They first tried things at a small scale to see how this would work

00:40:58.130 --> 00:41:05.130
and then what would be the feedback before doing a big rollout across the entire organization.

00:41:05.130 --> 00:41:13.090
They were quite certain, I'm pretty sure once they did that, that they had a solution that would work at scale.

00:41:13.090 --> 00:41:16.050
As some say, nail it before you scale it.

00:41:16.050 --> 00:41:27.020
So you have to nail it with one base, have one tribe operate like that, get some experience from it,

00:41:27.020 --> 00:41:33.140
and then do it with two others. And when you have three that are working like that successfully,

00:41:33.140 --> 00:41:44.460
you can start speeding things up.

00:41:44.460 --> 00:41:49.460
I thought that maybe Roscoe would want to ask something, but then...

00:41:49.460 --> 00:41:55.290
So yes, it was because also it was, but I guess you already answered it,

00:41:55.290 --> 00:42:01.440
that maybe unfix it is only for big companies or could it...

00:42:01.440 --> 00:42:08.570
So for the whole company, but also maybe for smaller units or teams maybe.

00:42:08.570 --> 00:42:14.540
But I guess you said like you can base, build up firstly the baseline.

00:42:14.540 --> 00:42:18.540
You can start anywhere. If you have a startup of just five people,

00:42:18.540 --> 00:42:24.450
you have a starting point. You have just one team, one team of founders

00:42:24.450 --> 00:42:32.460
who hopefully try not to become a traditional hierarchy

00:42:32.460 --> 00:42:36.460
once they have success and start scaling things up.

00:42:36.460 --> 00:42:43.490
They want to prevent things from becoming fixed.

00:42:43.490 --> 00:42:47.420
That's why I call it unfix, to keep things flexible.

00:42:47.420 --> 00:42:52.750
And yeah, so that applies to them as well.

00:42:52.750 --> 00:42:58.750
You begin with one team and then you have the building blocks for growing things larger.

00:42:58.750 --> 00:43:04.750
And then you'll have a base at some point of maybe, I don't know, 15, 50 people.

00:43:04.750 --> 00:43:08.750
At some point you need to decide, okay, we'll need a second base.

00:43:08.750 --> 00:43:11.750
And then these will have to coordinate things with each other.

00:43:11.750 --> 00:43:16.840
And then we'll have five of them. And hopefully that scales out.

00:43:16.840 --> 00:43:19.840
Horizontally instead of vertically. That is the idea.

00:43:19.840 --> 00:43:24.840
So it is not only a solution, I hope, for large enterprises.

00:43:24.840 --> 00:43:33.100
It is an easier solution even for the startups and scale ups who do not have legacy structure to get rid of.

00:43:33.100 --> 00:43:42.090
But they just want to prevent becoming a dinosaur with their organization design.

00:43:42.090 --> 00:43:46.090
I hope some are going to use Unfix for that as well.

00:43:46.090 --> 00:43:54.380
Maybe just one more question around this because I like this very much.

00:43:54.380 --> 00:43:59.380
And for people who really want to get started, which source, which book, which video,

00:43:59.380 --> 00:44:03.380
what would you say is the best thing to get started and get more familiar with the Unfix model?

00:44:03.380 --> 00:44:05.730
Where would you recommend to start?

00:44:05.730 --> 00:44:10.920
Well, the website. Because there is no book at this point.

00:44:10.920 --> 00:44:18.940
I just began half a year ago. And there's a lot of information on the blog.

00:44:18.940 --> 00:44:27.970
So web pages and the blog posts is what I have now and presentations that are available in some places.

00:44:27.970 --> 00:44:35.100
Also in the community, there's information available in the circle, community circle.

00:44:35.100 --> 00:44:41.100
So that's the that's the starting point. And hopefully at some point in the future,

00:44:41.100 --> 00:44:48.100
there will be a book that would be nice. But for now, I just focus on the short term stuff.

00:44:48.100 --> 00:44:57.330
From blog posts and presentation podcasts. Obviously, that's that's my focus for now.

00:44:57.330 --> 00:45:04.900
And are there already some companies applied this models?

00:45:04.900 --> 00:45:10.900
There are quite a few I hear that are playing with it, that are drawing pictures.

00:45:10.900 --> 00:45:18.900
But considering that I just launched the website in January this year, and we are now five months further,

00:45:18.900 --> 00:45:25.900
it is far too early to say that this or that company has transformed successfully with Unfix.

00:45:25.900 --> 00:45:31.000
That is probably going to change to take a while longer.

00:45:31.000 --> 00:45:41.000
For now, what I do is I go I look at some existing companies with case studies and I describe what they have already been doing.

00:45:41.000 --> 00:45:48.020
So, for example, Pipedrive is a company in Tallinn, Estonia, and they reached out to me and said,

00:45:48.020 --> 00:45:53.020
Hey, what you describe with Unfix is almost exactly what we have already been doing for several years.

00:45:53.020 --> 00:46:01.020
And then I interviewed five people and indeed it's super inspiring and super fun to see, to recognize the patterns.

00:46:01.020 --> 00:46:05.020
They just have other words for them. They call them tribes. I call them bases.

00:46:05.020 --> 00:46:11.020
They call them mission teams. I call them value stream crews, but it's the same, what they're doing.

00:46:11.020 --> 00:46:17.140
And other companies have reached out to me in a similar way that, well, this is actually 80%.

00:46:17.140 --> 00:46:22.070
of what we are doing over here. And we're super happy with that.

00:46:22.070 --> 00:46:26.140
So, and that's sort of obvious because I have not invented anything.

00:46:26.140 --> 00:46:30.140
I've just borrowed practices, good practices from other sources.

00:46:30.140 --> 00:46:34.140
And other people have already found the same ones and applied them.

00:46:34.140 --> 00:46:41.100
And I'm only the first one to collect them in a picture and give them nice colors and give them a name

00:46:41.100 --> 00:46:46.100
to make it easier for others to start doing the same thing.

00:46:46.100 --> 00:46:50.160
So that's the best that we have at this point.

00:46:50.160 --> 00:46:55.160
Companies out there are already sort of more or less doing something like this.

00:46:55.160 --> 00:47:03.100
They never called it unfix because it didn't exist five months before, five months ago.

00:47:03.100 --> 00:47:10.190
But they had the same insights and they're very happy with what they have built with those individual patterns and building blocks.

00:47:10.190 --> 00:47:17.860
Okay. Oscar, do you have another question?

00:47:17.860 --> 00:47:23.170
Otherwise, we should come slowly to the end.

00:47:23.170 --> 00:47:34.190
So maybe an ending question is if the listeners need to only remember one thing out of this conversation, what should it be?

00:47:34.190 --> 00:47:36.190
What should they take away from that?

00:47:36.190 --> 00:47:39.890
It's like Lego. It's not a framework.

00:47:39.890 --> 00:47:43.890
People keep saying, oh, yeah, tell me more about that unfixed framework.

00:47:43.890 --> 00:47:46.890
It is not the framework. It's a pattern library.

00:47:46.890 --> 00:47:51.850
We should try to get rid of that word as far as I'm concerned.

00:47:51.850 --> 00:47:57.110
That is I find that important individual patterns.

00:47:57.110 --> 00:48:04.110
You do the mixing and the matching to make sense, make something that makes sense in your context.

00:48:04.110 --> 00:48:07.110
But all the individual parts are optional.

00:48:07.110 --> 00:48:16.490
You can start with a blank slate, an empty table and then pick patterns from the box to your liking.

00:48:16.490 --> 00:48:23.920
Okay, yeah, then thank you very much and for the really inspiring talk.

00:48:23.920 --> 00:48:30.890
And yeah, then have a nice day and thank you for being in our podcast.

00:48:30.890 --> 00:48:34.050
Thank you Manuel, thanks Roscoe, it's been a pleasure.

00:48:34.050 --> 00:48:36.450
And have a nice day.
